      <text xml:space="preserve">[[Image:grafton_vt_highlight.png|thumb|225px|Grafton, Vermont]]
'''Grafton''' is a town located in [[Windham County, Vermont]].  As of the [[2000]] census, the town had a total population of 649.

== Geography ==
According to the [[United States Census Bureau]], the town has a total area of 99.5 [[square kilometer|km&amp;sup2;]] (38.4 [[square mile|mi&amp;sup2;]]).  99.4 km&amp;sup2; (38.4 mi&amp;sup2;) of it is land and 0.03% is water.

== Demographics ==
As of the [[census]][[Geographic references#2|&lt;sup&gt;2&lt;/sup&gt;]] of [[2000]], there are 649 people, 291 households, and 190 families residing in the town.  The [[population density]] is 6.5/km&amp;sup2; (16.9/mi&amp;sup2;).  There are 434 housing units at an average density of 4.4/km&amp;sup2; (11.3/mi&amp;sup2;).  The racial makeup of the town is 99.23% [[White (U.S. Census)|White]], 0.00% [[African American (U.S. Census)|African American]], 0.00% [[Native American (U.S. Census)|Native American]], 0.31% [[Asian (U.S. Census)|Asian]], 0.00% [[Pacific Islander (U.S. Census)|Pacific Islander]], 0.00% from [[Race (U.S. Census)|other races]], and 0.46% from two or more races.  0.77% of the population are [[Hispanic (U.S. Census)|Hispanic]] or [[Latino (U.S. Census)|Latino]] of any race.

There are 291 households out of which 21.3% have children under the age of 18 living with them, 56.4% are [[Marriage|married couples]] living together, 4.8% have a female householder with no husband present, and 34.4% are non-families. 28.5% of all households are made up of individuals and 10.7% have someone living alone who is 65 years of age or older.  The average household size is 2.23 and the average family size is 2.73.

In the town the population is spread out with 18.2% under the age of 18, 5.5% from 18 to 24, 24.7% from 25 to 44, 33.4% from 45 to 64, and 18.2% who are 65 years of age or older.  The median age is 46 years.  For every 100 females there are 114.2 males.  For every 100 females age 18 and over, there are 113.3 males.

The median income for a household in the town is $42,313, and the median income for a family is $48,250. Males have a median income of $31,346 versus $21,406 for females. The [[per capita income]] for the town is $23,617.  10.6% of the population and 6.3% of families are below the [[poverty line]].  Out of the total population, 11.0% of those under the age of 18 and 12.0% of those 65 and older are living below the poverty line.

==History and other features==
Frequently featured as a backdrop for commercials and movies, Grafton, Vermont is a charming, &quot;almost too perfect&quot; village with a unique history. Originally called Thomlinson, Grafton was renamed by its early settlers in a bidding contest. The winner, who reportedly paid &quot;five dollars and a jug of rum&quot; for renaming rights, changed the name to Grafton after his home town of [[Grafton, Massachusetts]]. 

In the early 19th century sheep raising became popular and multiple woolen mills sprung up along the branches of the Saxtons River. The town became a notable stagecoach hub for traffic across the [[Green Mountains (Vermont)|Green Mountains]] into [[Albany, New York]]. One inn from that era, &quot;the Old Tavern, was founded in 1801 and remains one of  the oldest continually operating hotels in the United States. 

The town suffered notably from losses during the [[American Civil War]] and local cemeteries in the village feature many tombstones of casualties from the [[Battle of Gettysburg]]. After the war, the community slowly lost most of its population and became a quiet backwater for nearly a century.

In the 1960's The Windham Foundation undertook an effort to restore the village. It purchased the Old Tavern and many residences in the area. Foundation also founded a craft cheese business and set up summer camps and a cross country ski center. The restoration efforts attracted new residents from metropolitan New York and Boston. 

In recent years the village has grown into a genteel, tourist-oriented community featuring a mix of artists, wealthy retirees and second home residents who live side-by-side with Vermont farmers, mechanics and entrepreneurs whose roots go back to the town's original founding.  Many of them can be seen at the village Green watching the Grafton Cornet Band perform on Sundays, just as they have for every summer since 1868.

